Z||000004||Oreif||10-08-2001||09:21 PM||||Just so everyone knows, That is the IMSA body kit. It will bolt onto a regular Fiero chassis. The body kit runs about $3500.00.<BR>If you ever wanted the "wide" body IMSA kit this is it.<BR><P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">          <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||IwannaIRM||10-09-2001||12:05 AM||||Ok, here's what I have found out.  It is a true IMSA body.  It's not an IRM body and it <B>will not</B> bolt up to the standard Fiero frame without major mods.  This is the actual IMSA body used when the car was raced in SCCA and Trans Am events.<P>The tube chassis is complete less suspension, running gear, wiring, tubing, etc.  So, in a sense, it is just what it states, "Complete tube chasis."  Maybe he should have said, Complete tube frame."  If you have the tube chasis book like the one that was sold with the Spice body awhile back this looks very similar.<P>This car would make a great SCCA or weekend racer.<P>Definately would be a rare Fiero if built properly.  This is the car that you throw in that true SD4 motor and have the 17"x12" wheels on the rear.  Racing suspension with racing caliber brakes all around.<P>Oooh, the adrenaline rush is starting....<P>Can you say, "Deep Pockets!"  <P>------------------<BR>AIM - SWags2933<P>84 Fiero SE (being modified)<BR>88 Formula||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mattyman||10-09-2001||12:29 AM||||Looks like a pile of junk||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||10-09-2001||12:52 AM||||    <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by IwannaIRM:<BR><B>Ok, here's what I have found out.  It is a true IMSA body.  It's not an IRM body and it will not bolt up to the standard Fiero frame without major mods.  This is the actual IMSA body used when the car was raced in SCCA and Trans Am events.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The body <B>CAN</B> bolt to a stock Fiero chassis. This is the DGP Body. Made by Diversified Glass. IRM bought the DGP body molds from them in (I think) 1993. It is not hard to do the swap. Somewhere I have the instructions to do it and it's very easy. It's like bolting on any kit car body. Whoever told you it won't bolt up is wrong and obviously doesn't know what they are talking about. This is the exact same body you get from IRM.<BR>Archie had a car with this exact same kit on it. (See Pics). Notice how the panel lines match the pics on Ebay?? The molding being part of the panels? <BR>It is on a stock chassis. See all the pics at: <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/imsafs.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/imsafs.htm</A> <P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.v8archie.com/mvc-169fm.JPG">   <P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.v8archie.com/mvc-166fm.JPG">   <BR>------------------<BR>               <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">             <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT><P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 10-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Indiana_resto_guy||10-09-2001||09:05 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>    The body [b]CAN</B> bolt to a stock Fiero chassis. This is the DGP Body. Made by Diversified Glass. IRM bought the DGP body molds from them in (I think) 1993. It is not hard to do the swap. Somewhere I have the instructions to do it and it's very easy. It's like bolting on any kit car body. Whoever told you it won't bolt up is wrong and obviously doesn't know what they are talking about. This is the exact same body you get from IRM.<BR>Archie had a car with this exact same kit on it. (See Pics). Notice how the panel lines match the pics on Ebay?? The molding being part of the panels? <BR>It is on a stock chassis. See all the pics at: <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/imsafs.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/imsafs.htm</A> <P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.v8archie.com/mvc-169fm.JPG">    <P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.v8archie.com/mvc-166fm.JPG">    [/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Oreif,<P>No, this kit will not bolt to a Fiero chassis. The body lines are the same, or very close. If you look at the pics you posted make note of the scoop area in front of the rear tires. Compare visual clues to the E-Bay shots and you may see what I mean. I drove to his place yesterday to check it out too. With a lots of work and some cash it would be a show stopper. The fiberglass panels, if taken apart and put in one box, could be lifted by one individual if he/she could get a good enough grip on it.<BR>The glass on the panels is less than 1/8th of an inch thick. A door panel weighs in less than 5lbs. at a guess.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||bHooper||10-09-2001||09:15 AM||||yes your right! the vents are different.<P>as for the thickness of the fiberglass, 1/8" is pretty thin isnt it?<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Red</B></FONT></FONT> 86 GT 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><B>Black</B></FONT> <u>86 SE Automatic</u>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||10-09-2001||09:22 AM||||If anybody has the Pontiac Performance Plus magazine that showcased the SD4 and Pontiac's Fiero racing program, this is the same type frame and body panels that were in that book. <BR>If I remember correctly, DGP made two types of "IMSA" body panels. The street panels were on Archie's IMSA. The thin racecar panels are what comes with this chassis.<BR>Suspension components to fit this frame have got to be ungodly expensive, if any are still around.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000011||IwannaIRM||10-09-2001||09:26 AM||||Orief,<P>Ok, not to sound like I am trying to argue over minor details but I have to ask.<P>The IRM kit and DGP kit have seperately molded rocker assemblies and use a stock door skin.  This kit has a completely different door that is a one piece mold with the rockers integrated into the door.<P>The roof appears to be a single molded piece that hasn't been glassed together.  Unlike a street Fiero that has a single roof that rests over the rear clip.<P>I was under the impression that the IRM/DGP kits came pre-drilled for ease of mounting on a stock body where-as this kit has no pre-drilled holes.<P>Just my observations.<P>I would agree that this kit has similar joints and looks but I still don't think it is a direct bolt-on as is the IRM kit.  Well, less final fitting that is.<P>Whoever buys it will have to let us know.<P>------------------<BR>AIM - SWags2933<P>84 Fiero SE (being modified)<BR>88 Formula||||reg||1
Z||000012||Oreif||10-09-2001||10:12 AM||||OK, I looked thru all the info I have. I stand corrected there are 2 types of bodies street and race. The one on Ebay is the Stage I Race Body. There is a Stage II race body that has exhaust cut out on the Driver side for the exhaust and the body is 1" shorter and 2" wider. The Street body differences are the scoops (as noted) and the sail panel mount is different to use the stock sail panel windows. (see Archies car and the race body)The sail on Archies is straight and the race body is angled to the body lines. The instructions I have are to put the street body onto the car which only uses "some" of the stock mounting points. (it doesn't use the mounting points along the side molding) All of the panels were designed by DGP and sold to IRM. I don't know if IRM has the race body molds.<BR>According to the DGP book there are 5 bodies.<BR>Stage I Race (GTU class)<BR>Stage II Race (GTO class)<BR>Stage I street (Coupe Body)<BR>Stage II street (Aero Notchback)<BR>Stage III street (Fastback body)<P>The Race Body sold for $2500 and the race chassis built by Baird and Trivette sold for $6500 thru Huffaker Engineering. (1985 pricing)<P>IRM sells the street bodies for $3500 for I and II and $4500 for III. (Don't know how acurate the prices are. These are from 1995)<P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 10-09-2001).]||||reg||11
Z||000013||Indiana_resto_guy||10-09-2001||12:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>OK, I looked thru all the info I have. <P><BR>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 10-09-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>How much info do you have Orief?<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Oreif||10-09-2001||01:40 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Indiana_resto_guy:<BR><B> <BR>How much info do you have Orief?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I have the original IRM catalog that has all the pics of the different stages, The Pontiac Performance Plus that shows how to build the entire race car, and photo-copies of the original DGP catalog with instructions on how to assemble the body kit.<P>I'm still trying to locate some other things I use to have back from the 80's. I know somewhere is a "Collision Manual" that showed how the Fiero body came apart along with part numbers and detailed instructions. It was in a plastic tan binder. I use to have a Kamai (sp?) catalog for their fiero kits, and I use to have a catalog from the folks that made the Fiero luggage. They had an entire catalog of items. <BR>The stuff I have isn't in the best condition, I have a few more boxes at my parents house to look thru. I had quite a collection if stuff back in the late 80's.<BR>||||reg||1
